<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! Quine Principles

A ''quine'' produces its own source code as output.
Lararium's target reaches past a literal quine — it runs as a ''self-describing system'':
one where the content graph contains the rules, schemas, and descriptions of its
own structure, accessible via the same capability surfaces used to access any other content.

The four quine principles define when the system reaches this target and how to measure progress.

''Theoretical grounding:'' TiddlyWiki (Ward Cunningham's original wiki principle —
"the simplest online database that could possibly work") meets Smalltalk's
live image model. In both: the environment contains its own description.
In neither: the description stands as a separate documentation artifact.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/p1-unified-store`>>

!! P1 — Unified Store

''Principle:'' System behavior descriptions and system runtime data occupy the same
store, addressable by the same scheme.

No "documentation tier" stands separate from "code tier." A meme that describes
what a filter operator does SERVES AS the filter operator's source of truth. A tiddler
that holds schema rules FUNCTIONS AS the schema used at runtime validation.

''Lararium expression:''
* `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/lararium/schema/tiddler-record` — the schema meme
  for tiddler records — loads through the same `resolveLarUri()` call that loads content
* `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/mu/pattern-integrity` — this very set of principles
  — resides in the same namespace as runtime configuration

''Violation pattern:'' A `README.md` that describes behavior no
meme mirrors. Documentation that can drift from runtime reality violates P1.

''Rule:'' Any documented invariant MUST have a corresponding meme with `invariant = true`.
The meme CARRIES the documentation; the documentation INHABITS the meme.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/p2-no-privileged-meta`>>

!! P2 — No Privileged Meta-Level

''Principle:'' No part of the system has special self-knowledge that other parts
cannot obtain through the same capability surfaces.

In a quine, the self-reproduction step uses the same output mechanism as everything
else. No "reflection API" works differently from the "content API."

''Lararium expression:''
* `filterTiddlers("[all[memes]]")` returns the same set whether called from a widget,
  a test, or the TW5 VM's own boot logic
* `resolveLarUri("lar:///ha.ka.ba/...")` works identically in browser, node, and worker
* `VmDebugSurface` on every peer — no peer has privileged debug access the others lack

''Violation pattern:'' A TypeScript-only introspection path that nothing
inside the wiki can replicate. If the wiki cannot ask "what memes carry this capability?" using
the same filter syntax as external code, the system violates P2.

''Rule:'' Any system introspection available to external TypeScript MUST admit expression
as a `filterTiddlers()` call. The filter layer serves as the reflection API.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/p3-causal-closure`>>

!! P3 — Causal Closure

''Principle:'' The content graph fully determines the system's behavior.
No hidden out-of-band configuration determines runtime behavior.

A quine's own text solely determines its output. If the output also depends on
an environment variable, a hardcoded path, or an external service, the quine
does not close — it stays partial.

''Lararium expression:''
* The meme corpus determines boot behavior (which memes have `invariant = true`,
  which have `mana ≥ 18`, which carry Heleuma source-sha256 anchors)
* The `lar:` URI resolver stands as the single gate — all content enters through it
* FPI-2 (wave vs water): behavior MUST stay independent of physical filesystem layout

''Violation pattern:'' An environment variable that changes which schema the validator
uses. A hardcoded hostname in a content reference. A feature flag outside the tiddler
store that controls rendering behavior.

''Rule:'' All runtime configuration MUST admit representation as a tiddler with a `lar:` URI.
The only exception: infrastructure secrets (keys, tokens), which MUST stay outside
the content graph for P3 reasons.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/p4-live-feedback`>>

!! P4 — Live Feedback

''Principle:'' System behavior immediately reflects changes to the content graph,
without a build/deploy cycle.

Smalltalk's live image: modifying a method changes the running system. TiddlyWiki's
save-to-self: editing a tiddler changes what the wiki renders, with no intermediate step.

''Lararium expression:''
* `MemeSyncAdaptor.onChangeset()` pushes CRDT deltas into the live TW5 VM — tiddlers
  update in the running wiki without a full reload
* Automerge's `DocHandle.on("change", ...)` event fires into `MemeProvider.handleChange()`
  — the CRDT serves as the live image transport
* `ReactionEngine` re-runs projections on changeset — UI updates follow content changes

''Violation pattern:'' A filter operator that requires a server restart to pick up a
new implementation. Schema rules that require a TypeScript rebuild before they apply.
Any "reload required" path for content that could live in the tiddler store.

''Rule:'' Any meme with `mana ≥ 18` and a Heleuma source-sha256 anchor MUST stay loadable
at runtime without a restart. The boot-time gate checks these fields; if they pass,
the gate prefers the meme over the compiled TypeScript equivalent.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/tw5-smalltalk-comparison`>>

!! TiddlyWiki vs Smalltalk

| Dimension | Smalltalk (image) | TiddlyWiki (self) | Lararium target |
|---|---|---|---|
| Unit of modification | Method | Tiddler | Meme (tiddler + TOML header) |
| Live update mechanism | Method dictionary swap | Wiki re-render | CRDT changeset + MemeSyncAdaptor |
| Persistence | `.image` file | Single HTML file | Automerge DocHandle (CRDT) |
| Distribution | Single-machine image | Single-device file | Multi-peer mesh (Beelay sync) |
| Introspection | `doesUnderstand:` | `[all[tiddlers]]` | `[all[memes]]` filter |
| Self-description | Classes describe classes | Tiddlers describe tiddlers | Memes describe memes via `lar:` URIs |
| Ephemeralization target | — | — | TypeScript → memes (Heleuma anchors) |

TiddlyWiki lacks Smalltalk's `doesUnderstand:`. `[all[memes]]` gives
Lararium's answer: a filter expression that returns the self-describing layer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-inviolable-kernel`>>

!! The inviolable kernel ~ what a self-hosting system refuses to let itself rewrite

''Every self-hosting system that survived runs a two-phase boot and keeps ONE kernel outside its own reach.'' Smalltalk lets a method redefine any method and never the dispatch algorithm that finds one. Emacs Lisp advises a primitive and never replaces the C body beneath it. Oberon versions a module interface and refuses a load that no longer matches. ''The mechanism that READS the extensible layer never sits inside the extensible layer'' — a system that lets itself rewrite its own reader has no way back from one bad write.

''Lararium's kernel reads as `resolveLarUri()` plus the parse-dispatch engine.'' A meme supplies grammar patterns, family contracts and templates ''as data'', and modifies nothing about the mechanism that reads them. That boundary lets P1's unified store stay safe: everything may live in the wiki precisely because the thing that opens the wiki does not.

''And the shadow layer names the shape for `lares/`.'' TiddlyWiki's read-through gives a user tiddler the power to override a shadow at a different address and never to delete one, with the shadow layer regenerating from disk at every compile. A carrier under `lares/` takes the same posture: ''override at a new address, never unmake the address beneath.'' That rhymes exactly with the retirement law — an address MUST keep meaning what it meant (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/meme#/retirement`>>).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/if-can-live-in-wiki`>>

!! The "If It CAN Live In The Wiki It MUST" Rule

This rule enforces P1–P4 combined, operationally.

''Test:'' Can this logic take the form of:
# A self-contained tiddler with `type: application/javascript` or `text/memetic-wikitext`?
# Accessible via `filterTiddlers()` or `renderTiddler()`?
# Updatable at runtime via `MemeSyncAdaptor.onChangeset()`?

If yes to all three: it MUST move to a meme. The TypeScript file becomes the
fallback only (Heleuma Stage 4: the meme holds authority, TS falls back).

''Current candidates (Stage 1):''
* `packages/lararium-tw5/src/filters/all-memes.ts` — filter operator body remains stable
* `packages/lararium-tw5/src/filters/toml-field.ts` — stable

Each has a small, well-defined body (< 30 lines). Each qualifies for a Heleuma anchor.

''MUST:'' Once a TypeScript module reaches Heleuma Stage 1, it MUST remain there or
advance. Regression (removing the anchor) requires documented justification in the
meme's `status-date` field.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/ipld-bridge`>>

!! IPLD Bridge

IPLD (InterPlanetary Linked Data) provides content-addressed data with:
* CIDs (Content Identifiers) — self-describing hash references
* Codecs — structured data (DAG-CBOR, DAG-JSON) linked by CID
* Schema layer — IPLD Schema for typed data structures

''Quine alignment:'' CID-addressed tiddlers stand as the fullest expression of P3 (causal
closure). Tampering with a tiddler addressed by its own content hash
changes its address. The content graph becomes self-certifying.

''Alignment with Keyhive:'' BeeKEM's capability tokens certify themselves ("Documents
identify themselves via public keys"). CID-addressed tiddlers in IPLD would complete
this: the content certifies itself at the data level, the capability certifies itself
at the access level.

''One URI, many substrates.'' The same `lar:` URI resolves against whatever substrate holds the bytes (a filesystem path, an Automerge DocHandle), and the resolver (FPI-5 trim tab) keeps that transparent to all callers.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/heleuma-anchor-system`>>

!! Heleuma Anchor System

The Heleuma system tracks ephemeralization progress from TypeScript toward memes.

A ''Heleuma anchor'' takes the form of a field in a TypeScript module's meme that creates a two-way
link between the compiled artifact and its meme representation:

```toml
# in the meme's TOML meta block:
heleuma       = "ba"        # stage marker
source-sha256   = "sha256:..."  # hash of the TypeScript source at time of meme capture
```

''Stages:''

| Stage | Description |
|---|---|
| 0 | Logic in TypeScript only; no meme |
| 1 | TypeScript file has meme; meme has `heleuma = "ba"` and `source-sha256` |
| 2 | Meme body contains compiled CJS; TypeScript fallback still active |
| 3 | Boot-time gate checks `mana`, `confidence`, `source-sha256`; meme preferred if all pass |
| 4 | Meme holds authority; TypeScript falls back only |

''`heleuma = "ba"`'': "ba" (Hawaiian: to carry, to bear) — the meme serves as the carrier
of the pattern. The TypeScript file stands as the material instantiation; the meme stands as the wave.

<<~/ahu>>

